<p>Thanks for reply!</p>
<p>Key summary:</p>
<p>For years the SCPs have kept to themselves, away in the hills, and aren't considered much of a threat (745 is Euclid). They also behave predictably- at dusk, separate groups make multi-colored light signals to each other across the valleys.</p>
<p>The &quot;Author&quot; (1st person, unnamed) and their partner begin their overnight shift guarding the containment area, a huge tract of land. That evening, the SCPs suddenly interrupt their usual light show and conduct a different kind of display. At the same moment, radio contact is lost with one of the other security teams. Author and partner speed off in their direction to find them.</p>
<p>They find them safe, but discover they've joined them in a radio &quot;dead zone.&quot; Unable to contact their base of operations, they decide to head back to their perimeter and hopefully escape the dead zone. They notice that the number of visible SCPs (points of light in the distance) are gradually increasing.</p>
<p>They finally get in contact with base, who confirms that they are to maintain the perimeter and await further instruction while headquarters comes up with a plan. They are also told a UAV is on the way to observe the area and provide a better idea of the situation. The number of lights continues to grow.</p>
<p>The UAV finally arrives, and sees what might be a lone, dim specimen separated from the group (though they're not sure what it is). The team is sent to investigate. They are apprehensive about the situation, as they feel they're under-equipped to face what might be a deadly predator, but being disciplined operators they press forward.</p>
<p>They gradually trek their way through the dark desert terrain, in search of&#8230; <em>something.</em> Author is the first to spot it- a faint light near the ground. As they move in closer, a sudden, overwhelming sounds strikes the team- a horrible shriek. It's a moment of panic and unbearable pain.</p>
<p>Author wakes up the next morning on the desert ground (lost time). They're picked up by a Foundation helicopter and taken to a nearby research site's clinic for treatment and recovery. Author does recall one incomplete memory from that night, a brief snapshot (I'll save that part, but if you combine it with other details from the tale, and the original file, you could draw a conclusion about what might be going on) Author is debriefed by multiple officials, but not told anything about the rest of the team or what happened with SCP-745.</p>
<p>At this point we learn the reason they're telling this story- they're about to undergo a procedure to attempt to recover lost time, and as part of that they have be amnesticized. They know their teammates are most likely dead or missing, and they want to save the only living memory of their last night.</p>

							</div>

								
					
