<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Emotional and symbolic, with undertones of mystery and light comedic moments</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> Traditional, with an account of the recovery, and addendum containing testing logs, and a few interviews.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A mysterious black, metal object resembling an hourglass appears in the sky above a foundation site and crashes through the roof. The object appears to consume the time of living beings near it, causing them to leap forward in time and age accordingly. The object ends up killing several researchers, but one seems to be unaffected. The object later even goes as far as to attach itself to him. This researcher then has to grapple with survivor's guilt, and must learn to keep moving forward, accepting both the good and the bad of the object being attached to him.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Additional&nbsp;Details</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Additional&nbsp;Details</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> (Ultimately, I want to tell a human story about a man initially struggling with guilt, but with the help of a friend, is able to accept the way things are and work to move forward.) After crashing into a foundation site, the SCP lands near a researcher. The anomaly then proceeds to scan him. After the anomaly is contained, the research wants to learn more about it, and is appointed head researcher. During a routine examination, the SCP activates, while our main researcher and two others are in the room. The two other researchers are killed by the SCP, but our main researcher is left. Later, the SCP permanently attaches itself to him. Through interviews with Dr. Glass, we learn he is suffering with survivor’s guilt and isolation. During subsequent testing, we find out that the SCP can allow the researcher to exist outside of time. (Time stop) The end of the arc will have the researcher coming to terms with the conflict he feels towards the SCP being attached to him and using it to help the foundation/the world, while leaving the origin and purpose of the SCP open ended.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> In addition to a relatable human struggle, throughout the article, there’s hints here and there that the SCP may have been intentionally created by an unknown group.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I’m hoping to expand on the backstory of the SCP in future tales or articles, as well as feature the researcher in some of my future articles. While I couldn’t explain every detail here, I have the main arc of the story pretty well thought out. Critique and greenlights welcome.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

